	<title>Shakespeare's Sonnets</title>
	<isbn>1903436575</isbn>
	<publication_year>1997</publication_year>
	<language_code>en-US</language_code>
	<description>T.S. Eliot once wrote that, "Shakespeare gives the greatest width of human passion," and it is this passion that has traditionally made &lt;i&gt;The Sonnets &lt;/i&gt;appealing to literati and laymen alike. Surrounded by mystery, these poems of devotion and jealousy, of a young courtier and a Dark Lady, have been the subject of endless speculation. They are highly mystical and at the same time highly honest; as W. H. Auden wrote, "...what is astonishing about the sonnets, especially when one remembers the age in which they were written, is the impression they make of naked autobiographical confession."&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Because they are witty, passionate, personal, and often ever bawdy, &lt;i&gt;The Sonnets &lt;/i&gt;stand as one of the greatest poetic tributes ever written to a beloved.</description>
